WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.299
Since its inception in 2008, The Marvel Cinematic
Universe has provided some of the biggest

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.620
and best moments in film.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.900
But not every Marvel movie is part of the
MCU.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.040
And the ones that aren't are often terrible.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.480
Here's a countdown of the ten crappiest movies
starring Marvel characters, going from best

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.800
of the worst, to the worst of the worst.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:24.960
The third and final installment of Sam Raimi's
original Spider-Man trilogy has one thing

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.980
going for it: it's the least bad of the very
worst Marvel movies.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.120
Overstuffed with too many characters and plotlines,
2007's Spider-Man 3 also veered straight into

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:37.980
camp with some truly weird and hokey scenes.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.740
And the best parts are basically just rehashes
of better moments from the first two Spider-Man

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:43.240
films.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.460
Watching this, it's pretty easy to understand
why the studio rebooted the franchise.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.860
A solo Wolverine movie starring the beloved
Hugh Jackman seemed like a no-brainer.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:58.680
And that's exactly what 2009's X-Men Origins:
Wolverine had: no brains.

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:03.449
For instance, the decision to include Deadpool
but literally sew his mouth shut so he couldn't

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:07.980
make jokes deprived the character of, you
know, his only actual purpose for existing.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:12.920
And the rest of the film was even less fun,
with an unrelentingly grim and tedious slog

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:14.320
through Logan's backstory.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.020
This is one of the rare cases where the first
entry in a trilogy was by far the worst.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:20.320
Just ask Ryan Reynolds.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:21.220
"hey!

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.160
It's me!

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:25.460
Don't scratch, just cleaning up the timeline!"

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:33.360
Speaking of movies that just aren't fun, Ang
Lee's 2003 examination of Bruce Banner's tortured

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.580
psyche was kind of the opposite of a summer
popcorn movie.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.700
Lee's Hulk also featured some very iffy CGI
at a time when the technology clearly wasn't

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.880
ready to do what they needed it to do.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:49.240
Eric Bana did fine in the lead role, but the
best thing that can be said for this one is

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:53.100
that it's failure helped pave the way for
Marvel to get the film rights back to the

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:53.600
Hulk.

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:59.280
Before Chris Evans played Captain America,
he was the arrogant yet suave Johnny Storm

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.800
in the first two installments of the Fantastic
Four franchise.

00:02:03.049 --> 00:02:07.800
So it's just as well that 2007's Rise of the
Silver Surfer was such a dud that they never

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.380
made and released a third film.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:12.340
Otherwise Evans wouldn't have been available
to play Captain America.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:13.920
What was so bad about it?

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.420
Well, beyond the boring CGI Silver Surfer
himself, the movie featured a terrible version

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.600
of Doctor Doom and an even worse version of
Galactus.

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:28.860
He should have been even scarier than Thanos,
but instead, he was a giant cosmic space fart.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:30.000
"That's gross."

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.540
Before Wonder Woman and Captain Marvel hit
the scene, the comic book movie world was

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:37.100
severely lacking in female-led films.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.020
Why?

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.260
Well, the craptacular 2005 flop Elektra is
a big reason.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:50.220
Nominally a spinoff of the 2003 film Daredevil,
Elektra featured Jennifer Garner gamely wading

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:53.959
through a convoluted and tedious plot about
an assassin for hire.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:58.240
It was kind of like John Wick, except really
bad in just about every way.

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.960
It earned a generous 11 percent critical rating
on Rotten Tomatoes, and absolutely bombed

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:07.952
at the box office, convincing Hollywood for
the next decade that fans didn't want to see

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.800
movies about female superheroes.

00:03:10.300 --> 00:03:10.940
But we did!

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:11.880
We really did.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.200
We just wanted those movies to be, y'know,
good.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:14.880
Ghost Rider

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:21.080
The 2007 stinkbomb Ghost Rider might not even
make the top ten worst Nic Cage films.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:24.140
But it's right up there with the worst Marvel
movies ever.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.060
How bad is it?

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:26.600
Signs point to terrible.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.540
Some would argue that the sequel is even worse,
but since that never would have existed without

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.200
this thing, the original still gets lower
marks.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:48.000
Thankfully, fans of the Ghost Rider character
finally got a decent version when he appeared

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.100
on the TV series Agents of SHIELD.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.060
Seriously, just watch that instead.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.579
Calling the 2015 reboot of Fantastic Four
a train wreck is a gross disservice to wrecked

00:03:58.580 --> 00:03:59.080
trains.

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:04.100
Director Josh Trank and the execs at Fox reportedly
clashed over the film from day one.

00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:09.159
They were allegedly so unhappy with what he
delivered that the studio commissioned rewrites

00:04:09.160 --> 00:04:10.960
and reshoots without his involvement.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:16.610
Trank claims they ruined his film, while other
reports claimed he was disruptive and erratic

00:04:16.610 --> 00:04:17.100
on set.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.260
The film was such a colossal failure in every
way that Disney allegedly fired Trank from

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:27.130
a planned Star Wars spinoff based just on
what they had heard about Fantastic Four before

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:28.120
it was even released.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:29.100
Ouch!

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:34.740
Adults today who can recall seeing Howard
the Duck when they were kids might be surprised,

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.900
upon watching it now, just how creepy this
movie truly is.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:42.480
And not just for the scene where an anthropomorphic
duck has adult relations with Lea Thompson.

00:04:42.620 --> 00:04:45.980
"Maybe it's not a man you should be looking
for."

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.680
"You think I might find happiness in the animal
kingdom, ducky?"

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.760
No, there's plenty of other creepy stuff in
this one too, which helps explain why this

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:58.980
was such a massive box office disaster.

00:04:59.420 --> 00:05:03.540
Still, Howard the Duck has its cult followers,
which explains why the character has made

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:08.951
cameos in MCU films like both Guardians of
the Galaxy movies and Avengers: Endgame, and

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:12.460
why Thompson herself has talked about making
a new Howard the Duck movie.

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:14.160
What a strange world we live in.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:20.640
Long before Samuel L. Jackson donned the eyepatch
and played Nick Fury for the MCU, Marvel's

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.250
top secret agent was played by another famous
face: David Hasselhoff.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:31.120
Yes, the Baywatch star and international singing
sensation played the lead role in the 1998

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.460
TV movie Nick Fury: Agent of S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.620
It featured incredible lines of dialogue such
as this:

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.220
"i'll get that vampire's blood if I have to
suck it from her neck!"

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.640
To say this version of Fury was a disaster
is an understatement.

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:50.740
It earned a dismal 16 percent audience score
on Rotten Tomatoes, and the series that the

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:53.080
movie was meant to launch never went forward.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.520
There's one person who thinks it was good
though: David Hasselhoff, who told movieline

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:58.380
in 2012

00:05:58.530 --> 00:06:03.460
"I love Sam Jackson, but you know… my Nick
Fury was the organic Nick Fury that was written

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.920
and discussed with Stan Lee before anyone
got in there to change it."

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.780
"...score one for the free world."

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:22.240
Here it is, the worst of the worst: 1990's
Captain America, which was so bad it's theatrical

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:24.900
run was cancelled before it could ever hit
theaters.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:31.180
Starring Matt Salinger, son of reclusive author
J.D. Salinger, this cinematic loaf has earned

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:36.580
a lowly 7 percent critics score and 16 percent
audience score on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.280
Why?

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.910
Well, the production values are non-existent,
the Red Skull was switched from German to

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:47.180
Italian for no apparent reason, and Captain
America, the Sentinel of Liberty himself,

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:50.970
twice ditches his friends by pretending to
be violently carsick.

00:06:51.020 --> 00:06:52.640
"would you please pull over the car?

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.100
I am going to get sick."

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.040
"Are you okay?"

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:04.980
All of America thanks you, Chris Evans.

